   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Predict--Observe--Explain (POE) in Science Education: A Narrative Review of Mechanisms</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Predict--Observe--Explain (POE) in Science Education: A Narrative Review of Mechanisms</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Variants</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Variants</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Evidence</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Evidence</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and Disciplinary Adaptations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and Disciplinary Adaptations</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>[-15]Predict--Observe--Explain (POE); science education; conceptual change; alternative conceptions; scientific explanation; instructional strategies</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Predict--Observe--Explain (POE) is an instructional strategy used across science-education contexts to structure learning around prediction</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Predict--Observe--Explain (POE) is an instructional strategy used across science-education contexts to structure learning around prediction</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">observation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">observation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and explanation. This narrative review synthesizes research on the canonical POE sequence and its named derivatives. It interprets the three phases as complementary mechanisms: prediction creates epistemic commitment and reveals prior reasoning; observation provides evidence for comparison and may</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and explanation. This narrative review synthesizes research on the canonical POE sequence and its named derivatives. It interprets the three phases as complementary mechanisms: prediction creates epistemic commitment and reveals prior reasoning; observation provides evidence for comparison and may</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but need not</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but need not</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">generate discrepancy; and explanation supports reconciliation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">generate discrepancy; and explanation supports reconciliation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">model refinement</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">model refinement</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and transfer. The review also examines major POE variants and critically evaluates evidence of effectiveness. Methodologically stronger comparative studies generally report benefits for conceptual understanding</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and transfer. The review also examines major POE variants and critically evaluates evidence of effectiveness. Methodologically stronger comparative studies generally report benefits for conceptual understanding</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">misconception reduction</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">misconception reduction</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">achievement</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">achievement</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">or higher-order skills</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">or higher-order skills</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">although findings remain heterogeneous because many interventions combine POE with simulations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">although findings remain heterogeneous because many interventions combine POE with simulations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">games</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">games</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">problem-based learning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">problem-based learning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">or additional phases</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">or additional phases</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">rely on intact classes and locally developed measures</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">rely on intact classes and locally developed measures</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and provide limited delayed or transfer evidence. The disciplinary analysis deliberately focuses on physics</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and provide limited delayed or transfer evidence. The disciplinary analysis deliberately focuses on physics</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">chemistry</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">chemistry</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and biology/biochemistry to examine how POE changes when relevant evidence is directly observable</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and biology/biochemistry to examine how POE changes when relevant evidence is directly observable</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">instrument-mediated</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">instrument-mediated</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">representational</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">representational</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">or model-generated. POE is most defensible when predictions require reasons</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">or model-generated. POE is most defensible when predictions require reasons</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">observations make relevant evidence epistemically visible</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">observations make relevant evidence epistemically visible</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">explanations provide intelligible alternatives</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">explanations provide intelligible alternatives</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and follow-up tasks test whether reconstructed ideas remain usable beyond the initial event.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and follow-up tasks test whether reconstructed ideas remain usable beyond the initial event.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
